.\" claude-herdr(1) — keep in step with main.rs COMMANDS, config.rs KNOWN and cli.rs.
.\" View:  man ./docs/claude-herdr.1      Lint:  mandoc -T lint docs/claude-herdr.1
.TH CLAUDE-HERDR 1 2026-10-21 "claude-herdr 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
claude-herdr \- coding-agent sessions inside herdr across your ssh machines
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B claude
.RB [ \-\-host
.IR alias ]
.RB [ \-\-debug ]
.RI [ command ]
.RI [ args ...]
.br
.B claude-herdr
.RB [ \-\-host
.IR alias ]
.RB [ \-\-debug ]
.RI [ command ]
.RI [ args ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B claude-herdr
.\" keep the agent list short; `claude agents --all` has them all
runs coding agents \(em Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, Gemini, Cursor, Copilot
and every other agent herdr supports \(em in
.BR herdr (1)
on the machines you reach over
.BR ssh (1).
A shell function maps
.B claude
(the default name; install.sh
.B \-\-name
picks another) to it, so plain
.B claude
opens the master dashboard.
The dashboard shows every machine's sessions, grouped and coloured, with live
machine stats, the selected
session's question and its recent output.
.PP
Commands that list sessions work across the whole fleet (the
.B hosts
setting), or across one machine with
.BR \-\-host .
Remote work is plain POSIX
.B sh
sent over ssh, and nothing is installed on the hosts.
Every ssh call uses BatchMode and a timeout, so a slow or dead machine never
blocks the others.
Cached data is shown at once and marked stale.
.PP
Everything that isn't a claude\-herdr command goes, untouched, to the local
agent the shell function stands in for: the
.B wraps
setting, Claude Code by default.
This covers its own flags
.RB ( \-h ,
.BR \-\-help ,
.BR \-v ,
.BR \-\-version ,
and for Claude Code
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-\-print ,
.BR \-c ,
.B \-\-continue
and
.BR \-r ),
its own subcommands
.RB ( mcp ,
.BR config ,
.BR install ,
.BR plugin
and any other subcommand its
.B \-\-help
lists, re\-read daily), anything run inside a herdr pane (when
.B HERDR_ENV
is set), and any command that needs a terminal when stdin isn't one.
.B "command claude"
(or
.BR "command" " \fIagent\fR)"
always reaches the agent directly.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-host " alias\fR, " \-H " alias\fR, " \-\-host= alias
Use this ssh alias instead of the default host.
For listing commands, this narrows the fleet to one machine.
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Log every external command, with its duration, exit status and stderr, to
.IR ~/.cache/claude\-herdr/debug.log .
.SH COMMANDS
Commands marked
.I (planned)
are designed and not built yet.
Until a command lands,
.B "claude help"
marks it as planned and running it explains that it isn't built yet.
.SS Sessions
.TP
.B claude
Open the dashboard: the Fleet view, or the Hosts view when no host is set yet.
Same as
.BR "claude ui" .
.TP
.B new \fR[\fB@\fIpreset\fR] [\fB\-\-dir\fR \fI/abs\fR] [\fB\-\-name\fR \fIn\fR] [\fB\-\-agent\fR \fIk\fR] [\fB\-\-worktree\fR[\fB=\fIbranch\fR]] [\fB\-\-tile\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-m\fR \fIprompt\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR \fIargs\fR...]
Start an agent in a new herdr tab named
.IR <agent>_<verb> [ :folder ]
.RI ( claude_ignite:px ", " codex_tune ),
then attach.
Names are fresh across every machine and the last 7 days, start with a letter
no other session uses, and avoid
look\-alikes.
.RS
.TP 16
.BR \-\-dir ", " \-d
Folder on the host.
It must be absolute, because
.I ~
would expand locally.
.B CC_DIR
works too.
.TP
.BR \-\-name ", " \-n
Session name: lowercase letters, digits,
.B \-
and
.BR _ ,
starting with a letter, at most 32 characters.
.TP
.BR \-\-agent ", " \-a
Agent:
.BR claude ", " codex ", " opencode ", " gemini ", \&..."
(default: the
.B agent
setting, or
.BI host. alias .agent
for that machine;
.B "claude agents \-\-all"
lists every one).
.TP
.BR \-\-worktree ", " \-w
Start in a new git worktree on
.I branch
(default
.IR <agent>/<name> ).
.TP
.BR \-\-tile ", " \-t
Tile N agents (1\(en9) in one tab, as an even grid: one
.B layout.apply
over herdr's socket (every pane starts at its final size), else splits.
.TP
.BR \-\-prompt ", " \-m
First prompt.
.TP
.BI @ preset
A saved starting point (see
.BR preset ).
Explicit flags win over the preset.
.TP
.B \-\- \fIargs\fR...
Passed to the agent.
.RE
.TP
.BR \-i ", " browse " [\fIargs\fR...]"
Fuzzy\-search the host's folders, then start a session in the one you pick,
or attach to the session already
running there.
.I args
are passed on as with
.BR new .
.TP
.BR ls ", " list " [" \-\-json " | " \-\-plain " | " \-\-watch ]
In a terminal, open the dashboard.
.B \-\-plain
prints the table.
.B \-\-watch
reprints it every
.B refresh
seconds.
.B \-\-json
prints machine\-readable output (see
.BR "JSON OUTPUT" ).
When stdout is piped, prints TSV:
host, name, status, cwd, pane, status\-since, title.
.TP
.BR resume ", " attach " [\fIname\fR | \fIhost\fB:\fIname\fR]"
Reattach to a session; this never creates one.
A session can be picked by name, tab label, pane id, or its 1\-based position.
With no name and several sessions, opens the dashboard.
.TP
.B last
Start a new session where you started the last one: same host, folder, agent
and arguments.
.TP
.B send \fIname\fR \fItext\fR...
Prompt a running session without attaching.
If the session is waiting on a question, this is refused: answer it with
.BR keys .
.TP
.B keys \fIname\fR \fIkey\fR...
Send keys, for example to answer a question:
.BR 1 ", " 2 ", " enter ", " esc ", " up ", " down ", " tab ", " space ,
.BR ctrl+c ", " shift+tab .
.TP
.B rename \fIname\fR \fInew\fR
Rename a session.
This updates the tab label (keeping its prefix and
.I :folder
part), the herdr agent name and the sidebar token.
.TP
.B close \fIname\fR [\fB\-\-yes\fR | \fB\-y\fR] [\fB\-\-now\fR] [\fB\-\-worktree\fR]
Stop a session and close its pane, after
.B close.grace
(5s; the agent keeps running until then).
Asks first unless given
.BR \-\-yes .
When a daemon or a dashboard runs the hub, it carries the close out and
this returns at once; otherwise this waits the grace itself, and ctrl+c
keeps the session.
.B \-\-now
closes at once.
.TP
.B close \-\-undo \fR[\fIname\fR]
Keep a session whose close is waiting out its grace (without a name: the
last close asked, wherever it came from).
.B close \-\-pending
lists them.
.SS Fleet
.TP
.B wall \fR[\fIname\fR | \fIhost\fB:\fIname\fR]... \fI(planned)\fR
Live tiles of sessions from every machine, through herdr terminal streams.
The named sessions are shown, or the marked ones, or all of them with the
blocked ones first.
Layouts are grid, main, rows and cols (see
.BR wall_layout ),
with
.B wall_max
tiles per page.
Keys are under
.BR KEYS .
.TP
.B tile \fIname\fR \fIname\fR...
Move sessions on the same machine into one herdr tab, as a grid
.RB ( pane.move
over herdr's socket, else its CLI).
.TP
.B hub
Open herdr's own window with every saved machine in its sidebar
.RB ( "ctrl+b w"
switches machine).
Offers to run
.B "herdr machine add"
for fleet hosts that herdr doesn't know yet.
.TP
.BR status " [" \-\-short " | " \-\-json " | " \-\-watch "] [" \-\-live ]
Counts of working, need\-you, done and idle sessions, and which sessions need
you.
Reads
the
.I status.json
written by
.B claude watch
when it is fresh; otherwise, or with
.BR \-\-live ,
polls the machines.
.B \-\-short
prints a compact line such as
.BR "\[u25B2]1 \[u273B]2 \[u2713]3" ,
for status bars.
.TP
.B watch \fR[\fB\-\-once\fR] [\fB\-\-interval\fR \fIS\fR] [\fB\-\-install\fR | \fB\-\-uninstall\fR] \fI(planned)\fR
Poll every machine every
.B watch_interval
seconds and notify when a session becomes blocked.
With
.BR notify_done=true ,
also notify when a working session finishes.
Never notifies on the first poll.
Notifications go through terminal\-notifier or osascript on macOS, and
notify\-send on Linux.
Writes
.I status.json
after every poll.
.B \-\-once
checks once and exits.
.B \-\-install
runs the watcher at login, with a launchd agent
.RI ( com.claude\-herdr.watch )
on macOS or a systemd user unit
.RI ( claude\-herdr\-watch.service )
on Linux.
.B \-\-uninstall
removes it.
.TP
.BR history " [" \-n " \fIN\fR] [" \-\-json ]
Sessions you started, newest first (default 30).
.TP
.BR recent " [" \-\-json ]
Folders ranked by frecency.
Piped output is TSV: host, dir, count.
.TP
.BR preset ", " presets " [" ls " [" \-\-json "] | " add " \fIname\fR \&... | " rm " \fIname\fR | " show " \fIname\fR]"
Saved starting points for
.BI "claude new @" name\fR.
.B add
takes
.BR \-\-host ,
.BR \-\-dir ,
.BR \-\-agent ,
.BR \-\-worktree ,
.B \-\-prompt
and
.BR \-\- " \fIargs\fR..."
.SS Hosts
.TP
.BR host ", " hosts " [\fIsubcommand\fR]"
Manage machines.
With no subcommand, shows the default host.
.RS
.TP 22
.BR list " [" \-\-json ]
ssh aliases from
.IR ~/.ssh/config ,
with the default, herdr and fleet columns.
.TP
.B pick
Choose the default host interactively.
.TP
.B set \fIalias\fR [\fB\-\-machine\fR \fIlabel\fR]
Set the default host, which is where
.B claude new
goes.
.TP
.B add \fIalias\fR \fR[\fIuser\fB@\fR]\fIhost\fR[\fB:\fIport\fR] [\fB\-i\fR \fIkey\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-default\fR] [\fB\-\-here\fR]
Add a Host entry to
.I ~/.ssh/config
(after making a backup), then set it as the default or add it to the fleet,
as
.B include
does.
.TP
.B key \fR[\fIalias\fR]
One\-time key login setup.
This is the only flow that may ask for a password.
.TP
.B include \fIalias\fR [\fB\-\-here\fR]
Add a machine to the fleet (the dashboard).
It gets a colour of its own.
Aleph looks at it first: one with no herdr or no agent gets
.B host setup
in a new terminal tab
.RB ( \-\-here :
in this terminal).
.TP
.B setup \fR[\fIalias\fR] [\fB\-\-agents\fR \fIa,b\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-herdr\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-agents\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-node\fR]
Everything a machine needs, asking before anything is installed: key login,
herdr (installed or updated, saved in herdr), then a checklist of every agent
Aleph knows (the ones there ticked).
The chosen ones install one after another, each checked (on the PATH,
.B \-\-version
runs, herdr sees it); a failure says why and offers retry, skip or the log.
npm agents get the current Node.js LTS first when the machine has none
(sha256\-checked, in the home folder, no sudo;
.B agents.node = off
or
.B \-\-no\-node
turns that off).
herdr's integrations are offered at the end.
When it's done the running Aleph (the daemon, an open dashboard) looks at the
machine again
.RB ( hosts.refresh ).
.TP
.B exclude \fIalias\fR
Remove a machine from the fleet.
Its sessions keep running.
.TP
.B color \fIalias\fR \fIcolour\fR
Set the machine's colour:
.BI # rrggbb
or a palette name (blue, mauve, teal, peach, pink, green, yellow, sky,
lavender, flamingo).
.RE
.TP
.BR doctor " [" \-\-json ]
Check this Mac (shell function, herdr, ssh), the host (dns, auth methods, key
login), the tools on it (herdr, and every agent found there with its herdr
integration; it fails when the default agent is missing), herdr's saved
machine, and the Warp/Raycast shortcuts.
Locally it also checks the agent the wrapper stands in for
.RB ( wraps ).
Exits 1 if any check fails.
.SS Setup
.TP
.BR settings ", " prefs " [" list " [" \-\-json "] | " get " \fIkey\fR | " set " \fIkey value\fR | " unset " \fIkey\fR | " edit " | " path ]
Every option, with its default (see
.BR CONFIGURATION ).
.B set
validates booleans, numbers and choices.
.TP
.B theme \fR[\fBshow\fR | \fBherdr\fR [\fB\-\-apply\fR] | \fBmachines\fR [\fB\-\-apply\fR]] \fI(planned)\fR
.B show
prints the machine swatches and session hues.
.B herdr
prints a herdr sidebar config that colours machine labels and session glyphs;
with
.BR \-\-apply ,
it writes that config into
.I ~/.config/herdr/config.toml
between
.B "# >>> claude\-herdr >>>"
markers, after making a backup.
.B "machines \-\-apply"
sets each host's herdr accent to its machine colour, then reloads the herdr
server.
.TP
.B shortcuts \fR[\fBinstall\fR | \fBls\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] \fI(planned)\fR
Generate a Warp tab config
.RI ( ~/.warp/tab_configs/claude_<host>.toml )
and a Raycast script for every fleet host, plus Raycast
.I claude\-status.sh
and
.I claude\-new.sh
scripts in the first
.B raycast_dirs
folder.
Only files containing
.B "# managed by claude\-herdr"
are overwritten.
.TP
.BR completions ", " completion " " zsh | bash | fish " [" \-\-name " \fIcmd\fR] \fI(planned)\fR"
Print a shell completion script.
It completes subcommands,
.B \-\-host
values, session names, presets, settings keys and
.B new
flags.
Example:
.B "source <(claude\-herdr completions zsh)"
.TP
.BR self\-update ", " selfupdate " [" \-\-check "] \fI(planned)\fR"
Update from the latest GitHub release of
.BR update_repo .
The release asset is
.IR claude\-herdr\-<target>.tar.gz ,
checked against
.IR SHA256SUMS ,
and the binary is replaced atomically.
.B \-\-check
only reports.
.TP
.B version
Versions of claude\-herdr, the local agent it wraps
.RB ( wraps )
and herdr.
.TP
.B help \fR[\fIcommand\fR]
Overview, or the details of one command.
.SH KEYS
Keys can be overridden with
.BI key. action = key\fR.
Press
.B ?\&
in the dashboard for the full list.
